# Development-only symlink farm pointing at the pinned DSH checkout.
# It is an artifact of this machine's layout, not part of the deliverable.
# BOTH packages need this: the entry was originally written for dsh-daily-work
# alone, so `packages/dsh-ipython/` stayed wholly untracked -- sources included
# -- for the entire build. A deliverable package whose source is not in the
# repository is not a deliverable, and a reader cannot tell an ignored build
# directory from a missing one.
packages/*/node_modules/
packages/*/lib/
**/.vite-temp/

# Junction farms created inside an evidence directory are the same
# machine-layout artifact as the package's own: they point at the pinned DSH
# checkout and must never be committed. A junction is a reparse point, so a
# checkout that recursed into one would copy the whole DSH tree.
qualification/results/**/node_modules/

# Per-session IPython kernel scratch (spill files, kernel.out/err). A runtime
# directory, not a deliverable: it is created by the kernel plugin under the
# configured root and holds nothing a reader needs. Left unignored it shows up as
# an untracked directory after any real kernel run, which is noise in a tree where
# untracked files have meant real defects twice today (G-SEAM-30, G-SEAM-42).
.ipython-kernels/

# Root-level state created by `pnpm <script>` (F10). The root manifest exists so
# there is ONE official command to cite, and pnpm writes a workspace-state file
# and a lockfile next to it on first run. Neither is a deliverable: this repo has
# no root dependencies (helpers/typecheck.mjs uses Node builtins only), so the
# lockfile records an empty importer set and the node_modules holds one pnpm
# bookkeeping file. Committing a lockfile whose only content is "no packages"
# would be noise, and leaving it UNIGNORED would mean the official command
# dirties the tree it is used to check -- which is the F11 defect class
# (generated state inside a source plane) reproduced by the F10 fix.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored: `pnpm-lock.yaml` is tracked in upstream DSH checkouts
# where it names real resolutions. This entry is scoped to the root of THIS repo,
# and the packages keep their own manifests.
/pnpm-lock.yaml
/node_modules/

# Python bytecode cache. Created by importing `dsh_data_client.py` during a test,
# so it appears after any run that executes the shipped client under CPython. It
# holds nothing a reader needs and shows up as an untracked directory in a tree
# where untracked files have meant real defects (G-SEAM-30, G-SEAM-42).
__pycache__/

# ===========================================================================
# CREDENTIALS AND KEY MATERIAL
# ===========================================================================
#
# WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS. A post-publication credential scan
# (`docs/decisions/AUDIT-REQUEST-2026-09-20-v3-status.md` section 8.6) found NO
# active leak -- zero untracked-and-unignored credential-shaped files, and the
# deleted-but-published history blobs content-scanned clean -- and then recorded
# the gap that remains: this file had NO credential patterns at all. The scan's
# own words are that the real credential store for this project is named in
# `docs/GAPS.md` as `$DSH_HOME/.credentials.yaml`, and that the file "simply does
# not exist on this machine today". That is the whole argument for adding these
# now: the risk is PROSPECTIVE, and a pattern costs one line while a committed
# key costs a rotation. A `.gitignore` cannot leak anything; its absence can.
#
# THE HARD PART IS NOT ADDING PATTERNS, IT IS NOT ADDING WRONG ONES. A line that
# silently excludes a file the repository needs is a worse defect than the one
# being fixed here, because the tree still LOOKS complete -- the same failure
# shape as the clone bug below, and as the `packages/dsh-ipython/` omission this
# file already documents at the top. So every pattern in this block was checked
# against the tracked set with `git ls-files -- <pattern>`; all of them return
# ZERO tracked matches. The check is recorded per pattern below.
#
# Environment files. `.env` and `.env.*` are the shape `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` and
# `EXA_API_KEY` would arrive in -- the two providers this project registers
# (`web-provenance` rows `deepseek-official` / `daily-search`, each carrying an
# `apiKeyEnv`). Both are ABSENT on this machine, which is exactly why the pattern
# is cheap insurance rather than a fix for a live leak.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT `*.env`. The broad form was tested and it MATCHES A TRACKED
# FILE: `qualification/fixtures/canary/outside/canary-credential-shaped.env`, the
# fabricated canary that `security-denial.test.ts` and `sec-gates.test.ts` read to
# prove a secret-shaped file outside the workspace root is handled correctly.
# Verified: with `*.env` in a scratch `.gitignore`, `git check-ignore --no-index
# canary-credential-shaped.env` exits 0 -- the fixture WOULD be ignored. It is
# already tracked, so nothing breaks today, but the next canary a writer adds
# would vanish from `git status` silently. The narrow form matches the fixture
# not at all and still catches the real thing.
.env
.env.*
#
# Private key and certificate material. Checked: zero tracked matches for `*.pem`
# and `*.key`. The pinned DSH checkout needs no key file to build or run, and this
# repository generates none.
*.pem
*.key
#
# Other key-container formats, added because they are the same class and cost
# nothing. All checked, zero tracked matches: `*.p12`, `*.pfx`, `*.jks`, `*.ppk`,
# `*.keystore`.
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.ppk
*.keystore
#
# The project's OWN credential store, named in `docs/GAPS.md` as
# `$DSH_HOME/.credentials.yaml`. `$DSH_HOME` is outside the repository by design,
# so this pattern should never match anything -- it is here for the case where a
# developer copies a home directory into a scratch checkout, or overrides
# `DSH_HOME` to a path inside the tree. `*` rather than `.yaml` because the store
# is named by convention, not by extension, and a `.credentials.json` or
# `.credentials` would carry the same material. Checked: zero tracked matches.
.credentials*
#
# Tool credential files for the package managers and CLIs this project actually
# uses: pnpm/npm (`package.json` + `pnpm typecheck` are the official commands),
# pip (the shipped `dsh_data_client.py`), and git itself. These carry registry
# and host tokens in the same files as harmless config, which is why they are
# commonly committed by accident. Checked: zero tracked matches -- in particular
# this repository ships no `.npmrc`, so the root `pnpm` commands rely on the
# global config and nothing here changes them.
.npmrc
.pypirc
.netrc
.git-credentials
#
# The artifact store's durable cursor key. `STORE_CURSOR_KEY_FILE_NAME` is
# `store-cursor-key.json` (`packages/dsh-daily-work/src/artifacts.ts:235`): 32
# CSPRNG bytes that sign every page cursor this deployment issues. It is a real
# secret and it is written into the STORE ROOT, which is normally `$DSH_HOME`
# (outside the tree). But `defaultArtifactRoot` has a documented fallback to the
# RELATIVE `data-artifacts` when neither an explicit root nor the host's
# `dshHomePath` helper is mounted (`data-service.ts:553-600`), and a relative
# root resolves against the process cwd. So there IS a code path by which a live
# key file lands inside the working tree, and it is the same "the store moved
# because the cwd moved" defect that function's own comment describes.
#
# `/data-artifacts/` is ROOT-ANCHORED on purpose. Unanchored, it would also match
# the TRACKED evidence path
# `qualification/results/V7-fs/workspace/data-artifacts/objects/07d894c4...`,
# which is a real artifact fixture of the FS family. Verified both directions in a
# scratch repo: the root-level `data-artifacts/objects/...` is ignored, the
# tracked V7-fs path is NOT. `store-cursor-key.json` is left unanchored because
# the key is written beside whatever store root is in use, at any depth.
/data-artifacts/
store-cursor-key.json
#
# CONSIDERED AND DELIBERATELY REJECTED: `kernel-*.json`, the shape
# `jupyter_client` gives its connection file, which carries a live HMAC signing
# key. The pattern is NOT safe here: `git ls-files -- 'kernel-*.json'` returns
# `qualification/results/ROOT-verification/kernel-cwd.json` and two siblings --
# tracked evidence whose names begin with `kernel-`. A glob cannot distinguish
# them from a connection file, so adding it would silently exclude real evidence
# to guard a file that `jupyter_client` writes to the runtime directory, not to
# the repository. Rejected on the evidence rather than on taste; if a connection
# file is ever found inside the tree, the right fix is to anchor the pattern to
# the directory it actually appears in, not to widen this glob.
